# =============================================================================
# restic exclude file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Matching rules (restic):
#   * A pattern with NO "/" matches that name as a COMPLETE path component at any
#     depth -- so "node_modules" excludes every node_modules dir anywhere.
#   * A leading "/" anchors the pattern at the BACKUP ROOT. With BACKUP_PATHS=(/)
#     that means the real absolute path, so "/var/lib/docker/overlay2" is correct.
#   * A multi-segment pattern like ".yarn/cache" matches that sequence at any depth.
#
#   !!! restic treats a line as a comment ONLY when "#" is the FIRST character.
#   !!! A trailing/inline comment becomes part of the pattern and silently
#   !!! matches nothing. EVERY comment below is therefore on its own line.
#
# Everything excluded here is a pseudo-filesystem, a cache, or regenerable from a
# lockfile/manifest. Persistent data is kept.
#
# THIS FILE IS FLEET-WIDE AND IS OVERWRITTEN BY deploy.sh. Do not hand-edit it on
# a host -- the next deploy silently reverts you, and the only symptom is a
# snapshot that quietly stopped containing something. Per-host patterns go in
#     $CONFIG_DIR/excludes.local
# which deploy.sh never touches. It is read AFTER this file, so it can also take
# a pattern back out: "!.venv" there re-includes what the line below excludes.
# See excludes.local.sample.
# =============================================================================

# ---- Pseudo-filesystems & volatile system dirs (relevant when backing up /) ----
/proc
/sys
/dev
/run
/mnt
/media
/tmp
/var/tmp
/lost+found

# ---- Swap (huge and pointless; lives on / so it IS caught by a (/) backup) ----
/swapfile
/swap.img

# ---- This client's own restic secrets: excluded by the script ----
# encryption-pw and config are excluded by restic-backup.sh, which builds both
# paths from $CONFIG_DIR and passes them to restic directly.
#
# Do NOT add them here. A path written into this file is correct for exactly one
# CONFIG_DIR, so on a host that sets a different one it matches nothing and the
# encryption password goes into the repository that password unlocks -- a (/)
# backup, no error, and nothing downstream that looks. Built from $CONFIG_DIR
# they are right wherever it points.
#
# ($DUMP_DIR lives in the same directory and MUST stay in the backup, which is
# why the script names the two files rather than the directory.)

# ---- Live MySQL/MariaDB data: captured via consistent dumps instead ----
/var/lib/mysql

# ---- General caches & trash ----
.cache
.local/share/Trash

# ---- Node / JS ----
node_modules
.npm
.yarn/cache
.pnpm-store
.parcel-cache
.turbo
.eslintcache
.next
.nuxt
.svelte-kit
.angular
bower_components

# ---- Python ----
__pycache__
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.tox
.nox
*.egg-info
.ipynb_checkpoints
# Virtualenvs: recreatable from requirements.txt / pyproject.
# On PRODUCTION servers you usually want these back -- a restore that already
# contains the venv is deterministic and works offline (no PyPI round-trip).
# Do NOT comment them out here; put "!.venv" and "!venv" in that host's
# excludes.local, or the next deploy undoes it.
.venv
venv

# ---- PHP / Composer ----
# Composer's cache when COMPOSER_HOME=~/.composer (the XDG default is under .cache).
.composer/cache
# Installed deps: recreatable from composer.lock.
# Same caveat as the venvs -- on production, keeping vendor gives a runnable
# restore with no "composer install" (network + correct PHP version) on the DR
# path. Take it back with "!vendor" in that host's excludes.local.
vendor

# ---- .NET / NuGet ----
# Global package cache (~/.nuget/packages); regenerable via "dotnet restore".
# NOTE: the bulk of regenerable .NET output is per-project bin/ and obj/, which are
# NOT excluded here -- bare "bin"/"obj" would also hit /bin, /usr/bin, node_modules/.bin,
# etc. If you need them, anchor them per host in excludes.local, e.g.
# /home/user/code/**/obj
.nuget

# ---- Docker (rootful; data-root /var/lib/docker) ----
# Exclude regenerable image/build state; KEEP volumes (your DB / app data).
# BuildKit / buildx build cache (the "docker cache"):
/var/lib/docker/buildkit
# Image + container layers (re-pulled / rebuilt):
/var/lib/docker/overlay2
# Layer metadata:
/var/lib/docker/image
# Container configs + writable layers + JSON logs.
# (Configs are regenerated from your compose files, which live under / and ARE
# backed up. The logs can be large.)
/var/lib/docker/containers
/var/lib/docker/tmp
# KEPT on purpose: /var/lib/docker/volumes  (named volumes = persistent data).
# Volumes holding databases you already dump are captured here too, but as a
# live (torn) copy -- the dump is the consistent source of truth. You may exclude
# specific DB volume paths if you want to avoid that redundant copy. That is a
# per-host decision, so it belongs in excludes.local, e.g.:
# /var/lib/docker/volumes/myapp_db
